1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:16,723
On the eighth of November 1519, a few hundred Spanish soldiers marched along a causeway across a lake into Tenochtitlan, one of the largest cities on Earth. Their commander was met by the emperor himself.

2
00:00:17,823 --> 00:00:28,020
Historians estimate the city held perhaps two hundred thousand people, far more than any city in Spain. Less than two years later, it lay in ruins.

3
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:36,949
Hernán Cortés had set out with about five hundred soldiers. The empire he brought down ruled millions of people.

4
00:00:38,049 --> 00:00:51,532
The real answer is not the old story of a handful of heroes and their guns. It involves an epidemic, tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, and a man prepared to do almost anything to win.

5
00:00:53,732 --> 00:01:04,719
Hernán Cortés was born in 1485 in Medellín, in western Spain, to a family of minor nobility with little money.

6
00:01:05,819 --> 00:01:19,045
At nineteen he sailed for Hispaniola, the island colony founded by Columbus. In 1511 he joined the conquest of Cuba under its new governor, Diego Velázquez.

7
00:01:20,145 --> 00:01:29,895
In Cuba he became a landowner and official, known for his charm and ambition. He fell in and out of favour with Velázquez more than once.

8
00:01:30,995 --> 00:01:44,627
In 1518 Velázquez chose him to lead an expedition to the newly sighted coast of Mexico, to explore and trade. Then he changed his mind. Cortés sailed anyway.

9
00:01:46,827 --> 00:01:57,195
He left Cuba in February 1519 with eleven ships, about five hundred soldiers, sixteen horses and a handful of cannon.

10
00:01:58,295 --> 00:02:06,551
On the island of Cozumel he found Gerónimo de Aguilar, a Spaniard shipwrecked years earlier, who had learned to speak Maya.

11
00:02:07,651 --> 00:02:24,376
After defeating a Maya army on the coast, Cortés was given twenty enslaved women. One, Malintzin, spoke Maya and Nahuatl, the language of the Aztec Empire. She became his interpreter, adviser, and later the mother of his son.

12
00:02:25,476 --> 00:02:35,908
Then he made a legal move. His men founded a town, Veracruz, whose council named him its captain, answerable to the king rather than to Velázquez.

13
00:02:37,008 --> 00:02:47,099
To stop supporters of Velázquez from turning back, he had his ships run aground and wrecked. The famous story that he burned them is a later embellishment.

14
00:02:49,299 --> 00:03:04,893
The Aztec Empire was an alliance of three cities, dominated by the Mexica of Tenochtitlan. It took tribute from dozens of subject peoples, many of whom resented it. Some neighbours, like Tlaxcala, had never been conquered.

15
00:03:05,993 --> 00:03:13,140
The emperor's envoys brought gifts of gold, hoping to keep the strangers away. It had the opposite effect.

16
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:25,205
Cortés exploited the empire's divisions. The Totonacs of the coast joined him. The Tlaxcalans fought him first, then became his most important allies.

17
00:03:26,305 --> 00:03:40,172
At Cholula, a city allied to the Aztecs, Cortés claimed to have uncovered a plot against him. His men and their allies killed several thousand of its people. Whether there really was a plot is still disputed.

18
00:03:42,372 --> 00:03:53,316
Tenochtitlan stood on islands in a lake, linked to the shore by causeways, with great markets, temples and canals. The Spaniards had never seen anything like it.

19
00:03:54,416 --> 00:04:07,323
On the eighth of November 1519, Cortés met the emperor, Moctezuma the Second, on the causeway. Moctezuma welcomed the Spaniards and lodged them in a palace.

20
00:04:08,423 --> 00:04:20,604
Why he let them in is debated. A popular story says he took Cortés for a returning god. Many historians now doubt it, and think the story was invented after the conquest.

21
00:04:21,704 --> 00:04:28,595
Within about a week, Cortés took Moctezuma hostage in his own city, and began to rule through him.

22
00:04:29,695 --> 00:04:42,175
In 1520 Velázquez sent a larger force to arrest Cortés. Cortés marched to the coast, defeated it in a night attack, and recruited most of its men.

23
00:04:43,275 --> 00:04:56,459
While he was away, his deputy, Pedro de Alvarado, attacked Mexica nobles during a religious festival at the city's great temple, killing many of them. The city rose against the Spaniards.

24
00:04:57,559 --> 00:05:11,020
Cortés returned to find his men besieged, and Moctezuma died within weeks. Spanish accounts say his own people stoned him as he appealed for calm. Indigenous accounts say the Spaniards killed him.

25
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:28,729
On the night of the thirtieth of June 1520, the Spaniards tried to escape across a causeway and were caught. Hundreds of Spaniards and many more of their allies died. They called it La Noche Triste, the Sad Night.

26
00:05:29,829 --> 00:05:37,147
Days later, the exhausted survivors won a desperate battle at Otumba and reached safety in Tlaxcala.

27
00:05:38,247 --> 00:05:53,073
Then came something no one had planned. Smallpox, brought to Mexico with the Spanish reinforcements, spread through a population with no immunity. It killed huge numbers, including Moctezuma's successor, Cuitláhuac.

28
00:05:55,273 --> 00:06:05,236
Cortés rebuilt his army in Tlaxcala. He had thirteen small warships built, carried in pieces overland, and launched on the lake around the city.

29
00:06:06,336 --> 00:06:21,141
The siege began in May 1521. About a thousand Spaniards took part. Beside them fought tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, above all Tlaxcalans, who did most of the fighting.

30
00:06:22,241 --> 00:06:38,177
Starving and ravaged by disease, the defenders held out for nearly three months as the attackers destroyed the city street by street. On the thirteenth of August 1521, the last emperor, Cuauhtémoc, was captured.

31
00:06:39,277 --> 00:06:48,216
Estimates of the dead in the siege run from tens of thousands to well over a hundred thousand. Many died of hunger and disease.

32
00:06:50,416 --> 00:07:04,773
Cuauhtémoc was tortured to reveal where the city's gold was hidden. Little was found. In 1525, on an expedition to Honduras, Cortés had him hanged on suspicion of plotting.

33
00:07:05,873 --> 00:07:15,623
Emperor Charles the Fifth made Cortés governor of the new colony of New Spain. Mexico City rose on the ruins of Tenochtitlan.

34
00:07:16,723 --> 00:07:29,587
The conquerors were rewarded with encomiendas, grants of Indigenous people who had to work for them and pay them tribute. Cortés himself was later granted some twenty-three thousand vassals.

35
00:07:30,687 --> 00:07:46,943
Over the next century, waves of epidemics, combined with forced labour and upheaval, caused central Mexico's population to collapse. Many historians estimate it fell by as much as ninety percent, though the starting figure is fiercely debated.

36
00:07:48,043 --> 00:07:59,925
Cortés's power faded. Rivals accused him of misrule, and the crown sent officials to rein him in. He was made a marquis, rich, but never again trusted with real power.

37
00:08:01,025 --> 00:08:14,231
He led an expedition to Baja California, then returned to Spain to press his claims at court. He died near Seville in December 1547, still petitioning the king.

38
00:08:16,431 --> 00:08:31,449
Historians such as Matthew Restall have challenged the old story of a few hundred Spaniards defeating an empire. It was also a revolt by peoples the Aztecs had dominated, who did most of the fighting and dying.

39
00:08:32,549 --> 00:08:45,712
Smallpox, steel, horses and Cortés's own nerve all mattered. But his allies were soon disappointed. The Tlaxcalans kept some privileges, yet they too ended up under Spanish rule.

40
00:08:46,812 --> 00:09:00,209
In Mexico he is remembered mostly as a destroyer. In 2019, Mexico's president asked Spain's king to apologise for the conquest. The Spanish government rejected the request.

41
00:09:01,309 --> 00:09:17,437
Malintzin's memory is contested too. For generations, malinchista was an insult for someone who sides with foreigners against their own people. Many now see her as an enslaved young woman who survived the only way she could.

42
00:09:18,537 --> 00:09:32,916
Cortés did not conquer Mexico alone. But he saw how to turn an empire's enemies, and an epidemic, into weapons, and he never hesitated to use them. A great city, and a whole world, ended because of it.
